<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>CSS Cascade Lab — Profile / Playlist / Hashtags</title>

<!-- ============================================================
     TASK 2 — INTERNAL STYLE BLOCK (combined here for a single file)

     ⚠️ NOTE ON THE CASCADE:
     In the original task you had an EXTERNAL styles.css and an
     INTERNAL <style> block. When combined into one file, we place
     the "external" rules FIRST (as a <style> block) and the
     "internal" rules SECOND — this preserves the exact same
     source-order behaviour:
       • Same selector, same specificity → LATER rule wins.
       • So .playlist-list ends up ORANGE (from this block),
         not peach (from the block above).
     ============================================================ -->

<!-- ---------- "EXTERNAL" STYLES (would normally be styles.css) ---------- -->
<style>
  /* ============================================================
     TASK 2 — CASCADE EXPLANATION
     ------------------------------------------------------------
     Q: Why does the second <style> block override the first?

     Both .playlist-list rules have the SAME specificity
     (one class selector = 0,1,0). When specificity ties, the rule
     declared LAST in source order wins. The second <style> block
     is parsed AFTER the first, so it wins → ORANGE background.

     If we swapped the order of the two <style> blocks, the
     peach color would win instead.

     ============================================================
     TASK 4 — INHERITANCE vs SPECIFICITY EXPLANATION
     ------------------------------------------------------------
     Q: Which font color shows inside .profile-card?

     body { color: #333 } sets a page-wide default (inherited).
     .profile-card { color: purple } targets the card directly.

     A rule applied DIRECTLY to an element always beats a value
     that is merely INHERITED from a parent — inheritance is the
     weakest link in the cascade. Also, a class selector (0,1,0)
     outranks the body element selector (0,0,1).

     Result: profile card text is PURPLE. Its <h2> and <p>
     children inherit purple unless they declare their own color.
     ============================================================ */

  /* ---------- Task 4: Page-wide font color (inherited by all) ---------- */
  body {
    color: #333;
    font-family: 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif;
    background: #f5f6fa;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 24px;
  }

  /* ---------- Task 1: Each section gets its own background ---------- */
  .profile-card {
    background-color: #d1f2eb;          /* mint green */
    color: purple;                      /* Task 4: overrides inherited #333 */
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    max-width: 500px;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
  }

  /* Task 1: playlist section background — will be overridden by the
     second <style> block below (Task 2) */
  .playlist-list {
    background-color: #fdebd0;          /* soft peach — loses to internal orange */
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    max-width: 500px;

    /* ⚠️ TASK 5 BUG (kept intentionally broken here):
       color: blue is set on the section, but the <li> elements
       still show black. Why?
       - The <li>s are NOT direct children of .playlist-list —
         they are inside a <ul>. color: blue is inherited by the
         <ul>, but a more specific rule (browser default, reset,
         or `ul li { color: #000 }`) overrides it for the <li>.
       - In DevTools you'll see `color: blue` marked as
         "Inherited from section.playlist-list" and losing.
       - The FIX is below: .playlist-list ul li { color: blue; } */
    color: blue;
  }

  /* ✅ TASK 5 FIX — apply blue directly to the list items */
  .playlist-list ul li {
    color: blue;
  }

  /* ---------- Task 1: Trending hashtags sidebar background ---------- */
  .hashtags-sidebar {
    background-color: #d6eaf8;          /* light blue — overridden by inline style (Task 3) */
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 20px;
    max-width: 500px;
  }

  /* ---------- Shared list styling ---------- */
  section ul, aside ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: 8px 0 0;
  }
  section li, aside li {
    padding: 4px 0;
  }
</style>

<!-- ---------- "INTERNAL" STYLE BLOCK (Task 2 — overrides above) ---------- -->
<style>
  /* This block is parsed AFTER the first <style> block, so it wins
     the specificity tie and the playlist background becomes ORANGE. */
  .playlist-list {
    background-color: orange;
  }
</style>

</head>
<body>

  <!-- ============ PROFILE CARD ============ -->
  <section class="profile-card">
    <h2>🎤 Alex Rivera</h2>
    <p>Indie producer • 12.4k followers</p>
    <p>Currently obsessed with lo-fi beats and synthwave.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PLAYLIST LIST ============ -->
  <section class="playlist-list">
    <h2>My Playlists</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Midnight Coding</li>
      <li>Sunset Drive</li>
      <li>Rainy Day Jazz</li>
      <li>Gym Pump 2025</li>
      <li>Focus Flow</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ TRENDING HASHTAGS SIDEBAR ============ -->
  <!-- TASK 3: The inline style below wins over ALL other background
       rules for this element (external light blue AND any class rule),
       because inline styles have the highest specificity (1,0,0,0).
       Only a stylesheet rule with !important could beat it.
       → Sidebar background renders PINK. -->
  <aside class="hashtags-sidebar" style="background-color: pink;">
    <h2>🔥 Trending Hashtags</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>#lofi</li>
      <li>#synthwave</li>
      <li>#newmusic</li>
      <li>#indieartist</li>
      <li>#beats</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>

</body>
</html>
